Q: Set off a scheduled event at a given time? A: <h2>Calculating time to elapse</h2>
<blockquote>
<p>I can't figure out how to get it to check the time</p>
</blockquote>
<p>In Java 8+, use the modern <em>java.time</em> for your date-time work.</p>
<p>Specify the target time as either a <code>Instant</code> (in UTC), or as a <code>ZonedDateTime</code> object (in a time zone).</p>
<pre><code>LocalDate date = LocalDate.of( 2026 , 1 , 23 ) ;  // January 23, 2026.
LocalTime time = LocalTime.of( 15 , 0 ) ;   // 3 PM.
ZoneId zone = ZoneId.of( &quot;America/Edmonton&quot; ); 
ZonedDateTime zdt = ZonedDateTime.of( date , time , zone ) ;
</code></pre>
<p>Capture the current moment.</p>
<pre><code>Instant now = Instant.now() ;  // Current moment as seen in UTC (an offset of zero hours-minutes-seconds). 
</code></pre>
<p>Calculate time to elapse as a <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs//api/java.base/java/time/Duration.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>Duration</code></a>.</p>
<pre><code>Duration duration = Duration.between( now , zdt.toInstant() ) ;
</code></pre>
<p>You may want to verify the duration is not <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs//api/java.base/java/time/Duration.html#isNegative()" rel="nofollow noreferrer">negative</a> (going backwards in time).</p>
<h2>Sleeping</h2>
<p>Now you need to wait. If your program has nothing else to do, you could just sleep the current (main) thread.</p>
<pre><code>Thread.sleep( duration ) ;
// … then do your work when execution of this thread resumes.
doSomeWork() ;
</code></pre>
<h2>Multi-threading</h2>
<p>If your program has other things to do, read on.</p>
<p>The <code>Timer</code> &amp; <code>TimerTask</code> classes are now legacy, as noted in their Javadoc. Instead use an executor service to execute your task on a background thread.</p>
<p>To get the delay we want before doing our work, use a <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs//api/java.base/java/util/concurrent/ScheduledExecutorService.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>ScheduledExecutorService</code></a>. The easiest way to obtain an instance is through the <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs//api/java.base/java/util/concurrent/Executors.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>Executors</code></a> utility class.</p>
<p>Define your task as a <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs//api/java.base/java/lang/Runnable.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>Runnable</code></a> or <code>Callable</code> object, or use a lambda. Use try-with-resources to automatically close your executor service.</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>try
    (
        ScheduledExecutorService set = Executors.newScheduledThreadPool( threadPoolSize ) ;
    )
{
    Runnable task = () -&gt; doSomeWork();
    ses.schedule( task , duration.toNanos() , TimeUnit.NANOSECONDS );
    // … submit to the executor service other tasks you need done.
}
// At this point all tasks have completed. 
</code></pre>
<p>Be aware that the current implementation of <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs//api/java.base/java/util/concurrent/ExecutorService.html#close()" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>ExecutorService#close</code></a> waits for 24-hours. If your task delay is longer, then you will need to instead adapt the boilerplate code <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs//api/java.base/java/util/concurrent/ExecutorService.html#method-summary" rel="nofollow noreferrer">provided in the Javadoc</a>.</p>
<p>Using executor services has been addressed many times on Stack Overflow. Search to learn more.</p>
